# Byte-truth for captured real-world fixtures.
#
# tests/fixtures/real/manifest.json records a sha256 for every captured file,
# and tests/fixtures/real/test_manifest_freshness.py fails the moment a file on
# disk stops matching its recorded hash. Git's line-ending conversion broke that
# guarantee: core.autocrlf=true (inherited from the PortableGit etc/gitconfig on
# this machine) stripped the CR bytes from two fixtures at COMMIT time, so the
# blob stored in the repo no longer matched the working-tree file and the
# byte-truth guard failed on CI while passing locally.
#
# `-text` marks these files opaque: git stores and checks out their bytes
# verbatim, applying no eol normalization in either direction. This closes the
# whole class rather than re-recording the two hashes that happened to trip it.
tests/fixtures/real/** -text

# JavaScript stays LF in the working tree, wherever it is checked out.
#
# Two guards slice deploy/cloudflare-worker.js on the literal "\n}\n" and take
# everything before it as a function body: tests/academic/test_notification_classes.py:312
# and tests/notifications/test_ntfy_wire_shape_guard.py:313. A CRLF checkout
# turns those into "\r\n}\r\n", the delimiter is never found, and the extracted
# body is empty -- so the guards pass on this machine (whose working tree is LF)
# and fail on a fresh clone. `text eol=lf` makes the checkout agree with the
# bytes the extractors were written against.
*.js text eol=lf